mc(5, `Hardy-Weinberg`, `A population is in equilibrium with 16% aa individuals. In one generation, no aa individual reproduces (zero fitness), while the others mate at random. The frequency of the allele a in the next generation is nearest to`, `0.29`, [`0.40`, `0.16`, `0.24`], `Start with q = 0.4, p = 0.6. Genotypes: AA 0.36, Aa 0.48, aa 0.16. Gametes come from AA and Aa only (total 0.84). Frequency of a = (0.48 &times; 1/2)/0.84 = 0.24/0.84 = 0.286, about 0.29. The value 0.24 forgets to divide by 0.84.`, `After removing a genotype, divide by the new total.`);
mc(5, `Hardy-Weinberg`, `A population has p(A) = 0.8. A group of 50 individuals starts a new island population and its p(A) is 0.5. The new frequencies of AA, Aa and aa (if in equilibrium) will be`, `0.25, 0.50, 0.25`, [`0.64, 0.32, 0.04`, `0.5, 0.25, 0.25`, `0.25, 0.25, 0.50`], `p = q = 0.5: AA = 0.25, Aa = 0.50, aa = 0.25. The change from 0.8 to 0.5 is the founder effect. The set 0.64, 0.32, 0.04 is the parent population.`);
mc(5, `Hardy-Weinberg`, `Population B (900 residents) has an allele A at frequency 0.5. One hundred migrants from population A, where A has frequency 0.9, join it and mate at random. The new frequency of A in the combined group is`, `0.54`, [`0.7`, `0.5`, `0.9`], `Weighted mean: (900 &times; 0.5 + 100 &times; 0.9)/1000 = (450 + 90)/1000 = 0.54. A simple average (0.7) ignores the sizes of the groups.`, `Gene flow is a weighted average.`);
mc(5, `selection`, `A trait has a normal distribution. Selection removes the lowest 10% and the highest 10%. After many generations, which will happen?`, `The mean stays the same and the variance falls`, [`The mean shifts and the variance is unchanged`, `Two peaks form`, `The mean and variance both rise`], `Removing both tails is stabilising selection. The mean stays and the spread narrows.`);
mc(5, `selection`, `Which of the following is the best example of disruptive selection?`, `Small and large beaks were favoured in a population of birds that eat tiny and huge seeds, and intermediate beaks fared poorly`, [`Average-weight babies survive best`, `Moths becoming dark over a century`, `Only one of two peaks being lost at random`], `Both extremes are favoured and the middle is disfavoured, giving two peaks. The others are stabilising and directional.`);
mc(5, `origin of life`, `Which of the following inferences about the age of life is supported by NCERT?`, `Life appeared about 500 million years after the formation of the Earth, which is about 4 billion years ago`, [`Life is as old as the universe`, `Life appeared 20 billion years ago`, `Life appeared only 1 billion years ago`], `The Earth is 4.5 billion years old. Life appeared about 500 million years after that, so around 4 billion years ago.`);
mc(5, `Darwin`, `Which of the following is a likely modern view that joins Darwin and de Vries?`, `Mutation and recombination give heritable variation, and natural selection changes its frequency`, [`Mutation alone drives evolution`, `Selection creates new alleles`, `Acquired characters are the source of variation`], `Mutation provides variation, which is random. Selection is non-random and sorts it. Selection does not create alleles.`);
