asr(5, `Hardy-Weinberg`, `Removing a harmful recessive allele from a population by selecting against affected homozygotes is very slow when the allele is rare.`, `Most copies of a rare recessive allele are present in heterozygotes, which are not selected against.`, 0, `When q is small, aa is q<sup>2</sup> but heterozygotes 2pq are far more numerous. Selection sees only aa, so only a tiny part of the alleles is removed each generation. R explains A.`);
asr(5, `selection`, `Natural selection can happen without any change in the genotype of any single individual.`, `Natural selection changes the proportion of genotypes in a population.`, 0, `Selection sorts individuals; it does not change an individual\'s genes. Over generations, the proportion changes. So R explains A.`);
